+# M4 MiniCPM-V 4.7 35B-A3B LES Coding Agent: prompt / tool orchestration A/B
+
+Date: 2026-10-14; device: base Apple M4 Mac mini, 16GB unified memory; SSD split-expert model. Engine: native resident `llama-les-native-server`, 4K context, `bank=128`, `ubatch=16`, `--swa-full`, `--cache-prompt`, LES lookahead off, temperature=0. All experiments used the same original six-test `SessionRegistry` Python fixture and guarded source-only edits. Script runs and output evidence are local to `/Volumes/ssd/les-eval/agent-bench`.
+
+## Experiment and actual results
+
+| Run | Model turns | Model tool calls | Preflight host actions | Wall time | Tests |
+|---|---:|---:|---|---:|---|
+| Native baseline | 6 | 5 | none | **362.4 s** | 6/6 PASS |
+| Compact tool descriptions + brief unittest errors | 5 | 5 | none | **405.0 s** | 6/6 PASS |
+| **Bootstrap source/tests/failures before model inference** | **3** | **2** | trusted host executes 2 read_file equivalents + run_tests | **273.6 s** | **6/6 PASS** |
+
+All 3 runs successfully executed `replace_text`, validated the real code changes using independent unittest, and issued a final model response. The standalone bootstrap run performed model tool sequence `replace_text -> run_tests -> final` after the host-collected read-only observations. No existing development repository was changed by the tests.
+
+## Why simple compression did not help
+
+| Measurement | Baseline | Compact | Bootstrap |
+|---|---:|---:|---:|
+| Aggregate prompt tokens (across turns) | 6,901 | 4,902 | 3,256 |
+| Previously cached tokens | 5,039 | 3,043 | 1,999 |
+| **Uncached prompt tokens** | **1,862** | **1,859** | **1,257** |
+| Generated tokens | 412 | 488 | 337 |
+
+Compressing raw `unittest` failures from 1,356 to 113 characters preserved the test names and errors, but did **not** significantly reduce uncached tokens because the baseline heavily reused context. Bootstrapping reduces expensive model/host round trips and uncached tokens (~32.5% reduction); this is a stronger lever on an SSD streamed-expert model.
+
+Bootstrap HTTP turn timings: 209.25 s (869 prompt tokens, 0 cached, 280 generated; `replace_text`), 46.48 s (1,172 prompt, 849 cached; `run_tests`), 17.49 s (1,215 prompt, 1,150 cached; final). **Single runs under different OS SSD/file cache conditions:** the 24.5% lower observed wall time than baseline is preliminary, NOT a stable/statistically controlled speedup. Every run completed with HTTP tool semantics; no Swap increase was observed during checks (~289MB already allocated).
+
+## Repeatable script and safety
+
+`bench_agent_bootstrap.py` is a self-contained host-side demonstration that ships with an intentionally faulty `fixtures/session/source.py` and `test_source.py`. It copies these into `/Volumes/ssd/les-eval/agent-bench/real-agent-sandboxes/native-bootstrap_repro-session`, runs actual tests, returns read-only observations to the model, and applies only `replace_text` edits to the copied `source.py` after a fixture-specific AST validation gate. It persists detailed prompts, tools, usage, tests and timings under `/Volumes/ssd/les-eval/agent-bench/`. It **does not** modify an existing user repository.
+
+```bash
+cd /Volumes/ssd/les-eval/llama.cpp-omni-git
+# Precondition: native server on 127.0.0.1:18793, as documented in README
+/opt/homebrew/bin/python3 tools/les-openai-bridge/bench_agent_bootstrap.py session
+# Overrides:
+# LES_AGENT_API_URL=http://127.0.0.1:18793/v1/chat/completions
+# LES_AGENT_BENCH_DIR=/Volumes/ssd/les-eval/agent-bench
+```
+
+**Safety limitation:** a path-restricted tool fixture and AST allowlist are *not* an OS-level sandbox. It is unsafe to run arbitrary generated source from unknown repositories as local Python without real process / filesystem / network isolation. This script supports **only** the included `SessionRegistry` fixture, and the AST gate is deliberately restrictive.
+
+## Next engineering direction
+
+1. Keep the original native server config. Bootstrap is the best observed orchestration strategy, not a model-weights speedup.
+2. Repeat A/B on several tasks and multiple random/cache orders before claiming robust performance gains.
+3. Tune the initial context: providing the entire test file incurs up-front prefill; explore failure summary + source first, then fetching tests only when necessary.
+4. For general-purpose Coding Agents, implement proper OS sandbox and per-tool execution policies before using model-generated code on non-fixture repositories.
+5. Optimize the **new-context** prefill and decode path / SSD expert access, not just raw Prompt size.
